140	<section id="s1" class="part">
141	<h2>Landed since the curator&rsquo;s last approval</h2>
142	<p>Two items, not yet read, oldest first. The curator read the whole page, up to item 40, at 06:25 UTC on 17 November 2026, so items 34 to 37 went. Both items landed on 17 November: item 41 at 06:29 UTC, and item 42 in two commits at 06:29 and 06:33 UTC, the second with the fixes after a cold read. Item 42 shows the two commits as one change against the text before them.</p>
143	
144	<section id="l41" class="part item">
145	<h3><span class="num">Change 41 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;Talking to the curator&rdquo;, &ldquo;An ask&rdquo;, the paragraph that begins &ldquo;Before you put a question&rdquo;, line 172</span>Draft 39 landed without its second cut: the switch-over keeps its definition</h3>
146	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">Landed text, against the approved words of draft 39; underlined, the words that the landed text keeps and the approved words cut. No approved word went. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
147	<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;An ask&rdquo;, the paragraph before the last, line 172</p>
148	<p>Before you put a question, run every probe that it needs. Say whether the path to the switch-over<span class="add">, the move of the compiled path onto the interpreter's library,</span> needs its answer. Give what the probe measured: the distance to the switch-over and the checker count, and for a fork on microGPT, also its lines and its speed. Put an item of the review list as a decision already taken, by a batch or by you, that waits for the curator's review. Never put it as a hold.</p>
149	</div></div>
150	<div class="note"><p><strong>A definition kept.</strong> Draft 39 made two changes to &ldquo;An ask&rdquo;. Its first change, to the last paragraph, landed word for word: a default is the option that stands unless the curator picks another, and the coordinator never tells the curator to take it unread. Its second change cut the definition of the switch-over from this paragraph, to pay for the words that the first change added.</p>
151	<p>The curator approved draft 39, and asked about the second cut: was it made to save space? Without the cut words, the sentence no longer said what the switch-over moves, and the curator found that important. The cut was made to save space, and for no other reason. So the landing kept the definition: the sentence still says that the switch-over is the move of the compiled path onto the interpreter&rsquo;s library.</p></div>
152	</section>
153	
154	<section id="l42" class="part item">
155	<h3><span class="num">Change 42 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, step 1, the paragraph after the steps, and &ldquo;The full boot&rdquo;</span>The boot prints the facts that POSITIONS cites, then reads POSITIONS whole</h3>
156	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, against the text before the change; struck through, the words that went; underlined, the words that came in. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator. The other steps of the boot do not change.</span>
157	<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, step 1, line 242</p>
158	<ol start="1"><li><span class="add">Run <code>explorations/coordinator/tools/positions-facts.sh</code>. It prints the FACTS entries that POSITIONS cites in numbered slices: with no argument, slice 1 and the number of slices. Print each later slice with <code>--part N</code>, to the last. Then read </span><code>POSITIONS.md</code><span class="cut">,</span> whole<span class="cut">. Read it</span><span class="add">,</span> in pages: it exceeds one page of the Read tool.<span class="add"> So you read the decisions with the facts that they rest on.</span></li></ol>
159	<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, the paragraph after the steps, line 251; the list of three after it does not change</p>
160	<p><code>FACTS.md</code><span class="add">, apart from step 1's entries,</span> and <code>INDEX.md</code> stay unread. Before you state a fact, or put anything to the curator, search them with <code>facts-extract.sh</code>. Never read INDEX whole. Read FACTS whole only at a batch landing:</p>
161	<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, &ldquo;The full boot&rdquo;, line 257</p>
162	<p><strong>The full boot.</strong> Only at the curator's word, read <code>FACTS.md</code> <span class="add">whole in place of step 1's script, </span>and <code>INDEX.md</code> whole<span class="cut"> too,</span> after step 1.<span class="cut"> It costs about three times the default boot's context.</span></p>
163	</div></div>
164	<div class="note"><p><strong>The decisions read with their facts.</strong> Since 16 November, the boot read POSITIONS whole and left FACTS unread. The curator found that the decisions were then read without the facts that they rest on, which is wrong, and asked for a script that prints those facts first.</p>
165	<p>Step 1 now runs <code>positions-facts.sh</code>, which prints the FACTS entries that POSITIONS cites: 47 entries today, about 29K tokens, in numbered slices. Then the coordinator reads POSITIONS whole. The paragraph after the steps leaves FACTS unread apart from those entries. A full boot reads FACTS whole in place of the script, since FACTS whole holds the cited entries too. The sentence on the full boot&rsquo;s cost was cut: its ratio, about three times the default boot&rsquo;s context, was measured for the old default boot, which read no facts.</p>
166	<p>A cold read then fixed the wording, in the second commit. Step 1 now says what the script prints with no argument, and how to print each later slice. The exception for step 1&rsquo;s entries binds to FACTS alone, not to INDEX. The full boot reads FACTS whole in place of the script, and the sentence on the cost went.</p></div>
167	</section>
